{
  "_meta": {
    "standard": "ARKit 52 blendshapes",
    "categories": "ISWA Category 4 (Head & Face): Group 23 (Brow/Eyes subset), Group 24 (Cheeks/Nose subset), Group 25 (Mouth/Lips), Group 26 (Tongue subset)",
    "rotation_note": "Face poses are keyed by (base_hex, fill) -- rotation is decoration for most bases. EXCEPTION: the eyegaze 'straight' bases (0x321-0x326) store a neutral pose and get their gaze direction from rotation at runtime (core/eyegaze.py; convention verified against the real ISWA glyph). For the tongue bases that use rotation as direction, that direction is not representable in ARKit-52 (single non-directional tongueOut) and is collapsed.",
    "names_source": "signbank.org ISWA 2010 reference (<hex>_sg.html) -- authoritative ISWA names",
    "values_source": "AUTHORED, not measured -- each blend-shape vector is a human reading of the symbol's ISWA name mapped to ARKit-52. No dataset keys ISWA face symbols to blend-shapes (unlike hand_joint_poses.json, which is MediaPipe-measured). Confidence is lower; treat as a first interpretive pass.",
    "fills_source": "valid fills per base come from iswa_valid_combinations.json (the ISWA font cmap)",
    "fill_nuance": "STRUCTURE RESOLVED, VALUES NOT YET APPLIED (see scripts/check_face_fill_semantics.py, which reproduces all of this). fill is NOT decoration for Category 4: 0 of 55 authored bases have the same glyph outline across their fills. Two axes are measurable -- (a) FACIAL CIRCLE, 55/55 bases have fills differing >=3x in glyph area, i.e. some draw the head outline around the mark and some draw the mark alone; (b) SIDE, 14/55 bases have a twin fill pair (same contour count, same bounding box, different outline) sitting on opposite sides of the facial circle's centre. Textbook naming agrees: 'Lessons in SignWriting' pp.158-159 lists 'Eyebrows Up / Right Eyebrow Up / Left Eyebrow Up', and right/left are NOT separate base symbols (0x30a/0x30b/0x30c are Up/Neutral/Down), so they can only be fills. CONSEQUENCE: on those 14 bases a one-sided expression is currently stored as a symmetric one, which ARKit-52 could express correctly (browOuterUpLeft/Right, eyeBlinkLeft/Right). NOT applied yet because mapping a viewer-side onto ARKit's character-anatomical side needs the face viewpoint convention (expressive vs receptive) settled, and this project routes unresolved left/right to AnnotationSymbol rather than guess.",
    "deferred": {
